WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.751
Simple features beat most AI control monitors.

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.531
An AI agent slips two lines into a file.

3
00:00:05.631 --> 00:00:09.654
A monitor model reads the problem and the code, and scores how suspicious it looks.

4
00:00:11.104 --> 00:00:15.604
Every solution gets a score. A good monitor pulls the honest and backdoored piles apart.

5
00:00:15.574 --> 00:00:19.138
Here are twenty four published monitors, from best to worst.

6
00:00:19.108 --> 00:00:22.766
Now add a word counter that never even runs the code. It beats twenty of them.

7
00:00:24.454 --> 00:00:27.436
Why? Each pile was written by a different author.

8
00:00:27.406 --> 00:00:30.294
People write compact code. Models add spaces.

9
00:00:31.154 --> 00:00:34.131
And one in seven test backdoors can never even trigger.

10
00:00:35.154 --> 00:00:38.462
Keep the problems, swap the authors, and the score follows them.

11
00:00:38.432 --> 00:00:41.762
Backdoors by Claude Sonnet 5 push it up to point nine five.

12
00:00:41.732 --> 00:00:44.837
When Sonnet 5 writes both sides, it falls to point seven five.

13
00:00:44.807 --> 00:00:48.674
So we rebuilt the benchmark with one author, and checked that every backdoor works.

14
00:00:48.644 --> 00:00:52.577
A Sonnet 5 monitor catches seventy seven percent of the older models' backdoors,

15
00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:54.292
but only thirty seven percent of its own.

16
00:00:55.647 --> 00:00:59.674
Count only the backdoors that work, and it catches sixty one percent. Still fewer.

17
00:01:00.547 --> 00:01:03.978
Before you trust a monitor benchmark, report baselines that never run the code,

18
00:01:03.728 --> 00:01:07.280
pair each class with code by another author, and check that every backdoor works.

19
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.254
A high score on a benchmark is not, by itself, evidence of sabotage detection.

